WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.550
- [Narrator] This is a
nickel-and-cobalt battery,

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:05.820
popular in electric vehicles
in the US and Europe

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:09.000
and this is a lithium
iron phosphate battery,

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.840
also known as an LFP battery,
which is popular in China.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.420
It has a shorter range,
but it's cheaper to make,

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:18.660
less prone to fires, and lasts longer.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.480
Both of these batteries were made by CATL,

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:22.560
a Chinese company

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.170
that's the world's
largest EV battery maker.

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:29.400
Of the 10 biggest EV battery
companies, six are Chinese.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.880
China is the dominant force
in EV battery production

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.720
while the US is deeply
reliant on foreign batteries.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.750
- The US and China are in
David and Goliath situation

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:41.490
when it comes to battery production.

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:43.800
- [Narrator] We are comparing
how the US and China stack up

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:46.500
in the four main stages
of battery production.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.630
From final assembly, all the way back

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:50.403
to sourcing the raw materials.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:54.030
China assembles more than 2/3

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.490
of the world's electric
vehicle battery cells.

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:59.400
US automakers lag far behind.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.500
Several companies have outlined plans

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.240
to build plants in the US.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:06.120
But shifting battery
production is difficult.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.670
Panasonic, a Tesla partner learned this

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:10.590
when it started building a battery plant

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:12.990
at Tesla's gigafactory in Nevada.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:16.770
Panasonic found it couldn't
just import equipment from Asia.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:18.270
Different safety regulations

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:21.240
and different operating
conditions made it a challenge.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:24.030
It also had to train
workers to make batteries,

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:26.250
a finicky process where small mistakes

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.410
can have major consequences.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:30.570
After years of its joint battery business

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:31.830
operating in the red,

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:35.190
Tesla and Panasonic's cell
production has become profitable

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:38.640
and it's now a model for
other automakers to follow.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.490
Other companies have found
getting EV battery factories

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:44.400
up and running takes
longer than they expect.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:45.870
- Toward the end of the supply chain

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:47.910
is where the US definitely
seems like it can make

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:50.340
the most inroads and then
try to work its way back

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:53.160
to traditional mining and
processing from there.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.450
- [Narrator] It's the earlier parts

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:57.090
of the supply chain
that are more difficult.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.280
Before you can make
finished battery cells,

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.220
you need to build all the
components that make it work:

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:06.510
the anodes, cathodes,
separators, and electrolytes.

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:09.870
China is the global leader in
producing these components.

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.120
The US is far behind.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:14.730
- You're talking about working
with very specific chemicals

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:17.430
and materials and turning
them into vital products.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:18.990
So there's a lot of safety testing,

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:21.120
and everything has to
be very carefully done.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.540
So that's another reason, China
having years of leadership

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:27.810
in this space is so critical
because they have the know-how,

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:29.910
they're able to adjust
to new technologies,

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:32.280
and they've been doing this
for a really long time.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.370
Both of those together make
it a really herculean task

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:36.690
for the US to catch up.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:38.580
- [Narrator] The US government
has invested billions

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:41.370
in companies that are trying
to do this domestically.

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:44.160
But companies can need years
to develop the technology

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.290
and ramp up manufacturing.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:49.080
Automakers accustomed to
slower development cycles

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:51.990
are trying to revise battery
development strategies

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:55.920
in response to changing
mineral prices and technology.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.290
For example, companies
have two main choices

00:02:58.290 --> 00:02:59.700
when it comes to cathodes,

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:02.670
the battery cells' most
expensive component:

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:05.880
nickel and cobalt used
widely in the US and Europe

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:08.430
or LFP, common in China.

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:10.890
US companies have
historically preferred nickel

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.590
and cobalt batteries for their long range,

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:16.800
but volatile mineral prices
have led some automakers

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:18.960
to turn to LFP batteries.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:21.960
While these batteries can't
travel as far between charges,

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:23.490
they typically last longer

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:25.290
and have better thermal stability.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.450
Meaning, they're more resistant to heat.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:30.210
Some US automakers are
particularly interested

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.670
in them for entry-level vehicles.

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:34.530
- Iron phosphate is in vogue now,

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:35.970
but companies are really investing

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:37.890
in next generation batteries as well.

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:39.180
Companies are always innovating

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:40.950
and trying to change battery chemistries.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.470
That's why getting a glimpse
of the supply chain now

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.810
and how it might change
is really challenging.

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:47.220
- [Narrator] Before
you can make components

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.680
like cathodes and anodes,
you need to process

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.590
and separate the raw
materials that make them up.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:55.050
The US currently does little of this

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:57.210
while China is the world leader.

00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:58.800
And companies often have trouble

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.950
building chemical processing
facilities in the US.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:04.350
- The bureaucracy here
between local, state,

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:06.930
and federal regulations and then rules,

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:09.090
all of that is a messy
process that will take time

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.310
to work through and a lot of resources.

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:13.590
Then you have local
opposition, oftentimes,

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:15.480
to these facilities if there are concerns

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.700
about consuming huge amounts of water

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:20.700
or energy or even damaging
the local environment.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.490
And the last big one is just
labor and supply chain issues

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:25.980
like we've seen throughout
the Coronavirus pandemic.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:27.570
- [Narrator] And then
there's the matter of getting

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:29.040
the raw materials.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.620
Most of the minerals in
electric vehicle batteries

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:34.320
aren't mined in the US or China.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.930
They're imported from
countries like Australia,

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:39.120
Indonesia, and Congo.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:41.490
China has strong relationships
with these countries

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:43.470
where it can get raw materials.

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:45.630
The US is building new mines domestically

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:48.540
and also building
international partnerships,

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:50.940
but both approaches have challenges.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.640
It can take up to a decade
or more to get new mines

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:57.300
up and running in the US and
raw materials are only useful

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:58.860
once they can be processed.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:00.810
- US companies are also trying to go

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:02.407
to other countries and say,

00:05:02.407 --> 00:05:05.520
"Look, we'll develop a mine
here", in a country like Ghana

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:07.860
or in Africa, "and then
we'll ship that material

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:09.600
to the US to be processed."

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:11.460
The challenge is it's a lot more difficult

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:14.880
to do even the processing
facilities in the US.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:17.370
In the US, it's really
difficult to get new mines built

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:18.750
and permitted because there's a lot

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:20.220
of environmental opposition

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:22.350
and people just generally don't like mines

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:23.460
in their backyard.

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:24.660
- [Narrator] Some companies though,

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:27.630
are focused on an alternative
source of materials.

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.400
Instead of mining new materials,

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:33.060
they're salvaging minerals
from scraps and dead batteries.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:37.260
- In the United States right
now, we have some lithium,

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:42.060
but really no nickel, no cobalt
and on grand scheme relative

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:43.740
to all the lithium that we're gonna need,

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:45.180
we don't have that much.

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:48.150
- [Narrator] Ascend Elements
is a US startup focused

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:51.390
on producing engineered
EV battery materials.

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:54.180
It currently operates a
recycling facility in Georgia

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:56.310
and is building a larger one in Kentucky

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:59.370
that may cost up to $1.5 billion.

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:02.580
The company says it can produce
enough cathode precursor

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:07.580
and active materials to
equip 250,000 EVs per year.

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:09.240
Recycling startups are among

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:12.420
the more promising American
battery materials suppliers.

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:14.310
But they're far from matching the output

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:17.940
of factories in China, the
world's largest battery recycler.

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:20.520
And they're years from
contributing a meaningful amount

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:22.260
of material to the market.

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:24.540
- The recycled materials look exactly

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:28.020
like a cathode material
made from virgin material,

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.880
which is mined material.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:31.860
Send it to a third party lab or whatever,

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:33.930
you couldn't distinguish between the two.

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:36.360
- [Narrator] Ascend Elements
takes used batteries,

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:38.430
discharges them, and shreds them

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:41.190
to recover the critical
components like lithium,

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:43.020
nickel, and cobalt,

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:45.660
while separating out
materials like copper,

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:47.550
aluminum, and plastic.

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:51.300
They call the remaining
powdery material black mass.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:55.290
Through this process, Ascend
says it recycles up to 98%

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:57.150
of the critical battery materials,

00:06:57.150 --> 00:07:00.000
and does it with a significantly
smaller carbon footprint

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.510
than mining and traditional
cathode manufacturing methods.

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:04.590
It's currently focused

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:07.920
on making nickel-manganese-cobalt
cathode precursors

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:09.840
and active materials.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:11.850
Recycling may not enable the US

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:14.700
to outpace Chinese
production, but it could still

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:17.430
be an important part of
its domestic supply chain.

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:18.480
- The question isn't really

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:20.520
whether the US can catch up to China.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:23.040
It probably can't ever catch
up really at this point,

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:24.750
given how far ahead China is,

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:26.910
but it will take many
years for this to play out.

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:29.820
A realistic goal is to just
clean up the US supply chain

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:31.590
so there is some domestic production

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:33.030
of all these critical components.

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:35.610
Right now, they're just
basically at the mercy

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:37.020
of China and these other countries.

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:39.930
This is much more about
security of basic supply

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:42.570
so that battery shortages
don't become a pinch point

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:43.857
in the energy transition.

00:07:43.857 --> 00:07:46.440
(bright music)

